<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uch Sharif</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Uch, Uchch</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uch Sharif is a historic town in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, near the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chenab">Chenab</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a> rivers. In the thirteenth century it was a political centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasir-ad-din-qabacha">Nasir ad-Din Qabacha</a> and a refuge for scholars fleeing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, and it became a great centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufism</a>. Its tiled tombs of Sufi saints, from the fifteenth century, are its best-known monuments.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uch-sharif.jpg" alt="Uch Sharif" width="320" height="597" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Usamashahid433; Haris Bin Saqib; Ali Shah Asani; Kumail Hasan; Musab Bin Noor; Fassifarooq · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:UchMontage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
